  'thread',
  'The TSX Rose 657 Points as Oil Fell Below $93, but the Relief Trade Has a Shipping Problem',
  'Toronto rallied to a two-month high as crude retreated on Iran peace expectations, even though attacks near Hormuz kept the physical supply risk unresolved.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The S&amp;P/TSX Composite advanced 657.17 points, or 1.84%, to 36,446.26,</strong><span> its highest close since September 4, according to Reuters.</span></li>
<li><strong>Brent settled at $92.57 a barrel, down 5.85%, while WTI finished at $86.85, down 4.38%,</strong><span> as traders reduced the geopolitical risk premium.</span></li>
<li><strong>Reports of tanker attacks near the Strait of Hormuz continued,</strong><span> making the oil selloff a repricing of expectations rather than proof of normalized shipping.</span></li>
<li><strong>The S&amp;P 500 rose 2.35% to 7,549.94 and the Nasdaq gained 2.74% to 25,253.14,</strong><span> showing that the relief trade extended beyond Canadian equities.</span></li>
<li><strong>Canada’s October employment fell by 4,700 and the unemployment rate reached 6.7%,</strong><span> leaving the domestic economic picture less buoyant than Monday’s equity tape.</span></li></ul>',
  '<p>Monday''s market reaction offered a deceptively tidy narrative: oil fell, equities rose and Toronto outperformed as the threat of further U.S. strikes on Iran appeared to recede. The S&amp;P/TSX Composite climbed 657.17 points, or 1.84%, to 36,446.26, its highest closing level since September 4. Brent crude settled at $92.57, down 5.85%, and U.S. West Texas Intermediate ended at $86.85, down 4.38%. Those numbers describe a powerful repricing. They do not establish that the underlying supply disruption is over.</p>
<h2>Oil Fell on a Political Signal, Not a Shipping Clearance</h2>
<p>Crude had already fallen sharply during the preceding week. Monday extended that decline after President Donald Trump indicated that fresh attacks on Iran were unlikely. A lower perceived probability of escalation can take dollars out of a barrel of oil very quickly, even before a single vessel changes its route. Yet reports of attacks involving tankers near the Strait of Hormuz persisted. That distinction matters: futures prices incorporate changing expectations about future supply, while shipping availability, insurance costs and physical delivery schedules can take longer to normalize.</p>
<p>The price decline therefore contains two competing messages. It reduces near-term inflation pressure if sustained, but it also signals how much of the recent oil price was a risk premium vulnerable to headlines. A renewed attack or a failed diplomatic initiative could reverse part of the move without any change in global demand.</p>
<h2>Toronto''s Rally Was Broader Than an Energy Trade</h2>
<p>Reuters reported that miners and industrial companies helped lead the TSX higher. That sector mix is significant. An oil-heavy benchmark does not mechanically rise when crude falls; Canadian energy producers may face lower revenue expectations even as transportation, industrial and interest-sensitive businesses benefit from cheaper fuel and a less threatening inflation outlook. Monday''s advance was consistent with investors favouring the second effect across the broader index.</p>
<p>The move was not uniquely Canadian. The S&amp;P 500 gained 2.35% to 7,549.94 and the Nasdaq Composite rose 2.74% to 25,253.14. That breadth makes the session look like a global risk-appetite rebound rather than a narrow repricing of Canadian oil producers. It also raises the bar for the next trading day: gains built on geopolitical relief need either calmer news or improving earnings and economic fundamentals to persist.</p><div class="hdq-chart" style="margin:22px 0;border:1px solid #e5e5e5;padding:14px;font-family:Arial,sans-serif"><div style="font-size:12px;font-weight:700;color:#1a3560">MONDAY''S REPRICING</div><div style="font-size:10px;color:#666;margin:5px 0 10px">November 9, 2026, closing percentage changes</div><div id="hdq-thread-chart-155"></div><script>(function(){var host=document.getElementById(''hdq-thread-chart-155'');if(!host)return;var ns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'',data=[[''TSX'',1.84],[''S&P 500'',2.35],[''Nasdaq'',2.74],[''Brent'',-5.85],[''WTI'',-4.38]],svg=document.createElementNS(ns,''svg'');svg.setAttribute(''viewBox'',''0 0 640 238'');svg.setAttribute(''role'',''img'');svg.setAttribute(''aria-label'',''TSX up 1.84 percent, S and P 500 up 2.35 percent, Nasdaq up 2.74 percent, Brent down 5.85 percent, WTI down 4.38 percent'');svg.style.width=''100%'';function add(tag,attrs,txt){var el=document.createElementNS(ns,tag);Object.keys(attrs).forEach(function(k){el.setAttribute(k,attrs[k])});if(txt!==undefined)el.textContent=txt;svg.appendChild(el)}var zero=370,scale=34;add(''line'',{x1:zero,y1:14,x2:zero,y2:219,stroke:''#aaa'',''stroke-width'':1});data.forEach(function(d,i){var y=22+i*43,x=d[1]>=0?zero+2:zero+d[1]*scale,w=Math.abs(d[1])*scale;add(''text'',{x:8,y:y+15,''font-size'':13,fill:''#333''},d[0]);add(''rect'',{x:x,y:y,width:w,height:22,fill:d[1]>=0?''#274b70'':''#a66c4c'',rx:2});add(''text'',{x:d[1]>=0?x+w+7:x-7,y:y+16,''text-anchor'':d[1]>=0?''start'':''end'',''font-size'':12,''font-weight'':''bold'',fill:''#333''},(d[1]>0?''+'':'''')+d[1].toFixed(2)+''%'')});host.appendChild(svg)})();</script><div style="font-size:10px;color:#777;margin-top:8px;font-style:italic">Sources: Reuters, November 9, 2026, Toronto and U.S. market closing reports; Reuters oil settlement coverage. Returns are same-day percentage changes, not cumulative performance. | hdq.ca</div></div><h2>The Domestic Economy Has Not Joined the Celebration</h2>
<p>Statistics Canada''s October labour-force report, released Friday, recorded a decline of 4,700 jobs and a rise in unemployment to 6.7%. The employment rate stood at 60.5%. These are not catastrophic monthly figures, but they complicate the idea that a stronger stock market means the Canadian economy is accelerating. Equities price expected future earnings and discount rates; the labour survey measures employment conditions households are experiencing now.</p>
<p>For Canadian advisors, the useful distinction is between a change in market price and a change in client capacity. Lower oil may eventually help inflation and consumer budgets, but one session of cheaper crude does not restore lost employment income or settle a household''s liquidity needs. A portfolio that benefited from Monday''s rally may still require rebalancing, not a larger risk allocation.</p>
<p>The next test is whether shipping conditions improve alongside the oil price. If tanker incidents continue while crude remains below $93, markets are expressing confidence that diplomacy will prevail over physical disruption. If oil rebounds and equities give back the rally, Monday will look less like a new trend and more like the latest turn in a volatile geopolitical trade.</p>',
